    "knowledgeBaseRetrievedContents": [
      "{FileName: KnightLaw_FAQs.md}\n{H1: Frequently Asked Questions (Initial Intake Call)}\n# Frequently Asked Questions (Initial Intake Call)\n### Where are you located?\n\n**A:** We have four offices strategically located across California, allowing us to serve clients throughout the state.\n### What do we do?\n\n**A:** We help consumers get out of their defective vehicles and get back the money paid in regard to their sales contract/lease agreement. The California Lemon Law makes it favorable to the consumer because it requires the car manufacturer to replace or repurchase your vehicle when they fail to conform it to warranty after a reasonable number of repair attempts.",
      "{FileName: knight-law-group-faqs-14-07-2026.md}\nMaintaining regular and up-to-date payments is important, as it establishes a record that reflects your responsible handling of your vehicle-related obligations.\n---\n18. **Can I continue driving the car?**\n**A:** Yes, throughout the litigation of your case you can continue to drive the vehicle as expected.\n---\n19. **Would you still be able to help me if I am planning to move to another state in a few months?**\n**A:** If we can proceed with a lawsuit, we absolutely can still help you if you relocate out of California.\nHowever, it's important to be willing to travel to California if circumstances require it.\n---\n20. **I heard I need three repair attempts to qualify.**\n**A:** That's not always the case.\nWith the California Lemon Law, what's crucial is a reasonable number of repair attempts.\nThis varies depending on the specific vehicle, the defect in questions and or the repair representations.\nTo determine whether we can move forward, we'll need to review the repair order history.\n---\n21. **I have repairs, but they were not at the dealership, will that be a problem?**\n**A:** If your repair attempts were not at a dealership that is the same brand as your vehicle, we will not be able to help you.\n---\n22. **I have the repair orders, but they do not state the issues that I brought it in for.**\n**A:** i. *If the vehicle is no longer under warranty:* If the repair documents don't clearly state the issues, you brought the vehicle in for, we may be unable to assist.\n*ii.\nIf vehicle still under warranty:* Given that your vehicle is still covered by warranty, I suggest setting up an appointment at your nearest dealership to begin establishing a history of visits.\n---\n23. **I bought my Tesla/Rivian/Lucid NEW; can you assist me?**\n**A.** The Tesla cases we’ve had in the past have gone to arbitration because the consumer, at the time of purchase, agreed to go directly into arbitration with Tesla over any conflict.\nWe do not go to arbitration, we file lawsuits.",
      "{FileName: knight-law-group-faqs-14-07-2026.md}\n7. **The dealership does not want to give me my repair documents.\nCan you request the repair documents on my behalf?**\n**A:** If you're unable to obtain the necessary repair documents, we can attempt to request them on your behalf.\n---\n8. **Why do you need to see my repair documents?**\n**A:** The service records are the evidence of repair attempts for your case.\nThey give us a detailed timeline of your dealership visits and the work performed on the vehicle.\nThey document the steps the dealer has taken to address issues, which helps determine how to proceed.\n---\n9. **How do I send in my repair documents\\*?**\n**A:** There are three ways you can submit your repair documents to us.\nText: 800-243-8711, Fax: (855) 355-8422, Email: [intakedocs@knightlaw.com](mailto:intakedocs@knightlaw.com)\n\\*If the agent is capable to collect documents, then the instructions should be to receive repair documents via the channel where the lead engaged with (usually SMS or Email)\n---\n10. **What is the fee that I will be charged?**\n**A:** The benefit of the California Lemon Law is that the car manufacturer is responsible for paying all attorney’s fees and costs.\nYou do not send our firm any money or receive a bill from us.\n---\n11. **I got an offer from the car manufacturer.\nShould I accept it?**\n**A:** Before you accept an offer, we can review it to determine if they are reimbursing all the money you are owed.\nFor us to review the offer, we will need a copy of your repair orders, a copy of the sales contract/lease agreement, and the actual offer.\nOnce everything is received, we can have an answer typically within (1) business day.\n---\n12. **How long does it take to review the repair documents?**\n**A:** We can review your documents within 24-48 hours.\n---\n13. **I think I had a lemon, but I sold the car.\nCan I still pursue my California Lemon Law rights?**\n**A:** It depends on who your car manufacturer is."
    ],
